  "configuration_id": "config_009",
  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so we have zero information about which design choices (modality, profile setting, encoder, aggregation, fusion) actually matter or whether added complexity over a simple baseline provides any benefit. The most critical unknown is the performance floor of the simplest viable model. 2) What this tests: config_009 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) is the cleanest, most interpretable baseline—standard PCA on RNA with a single profile and no image modality, aggregation, or fusion. It establishes a reference performance level against which every more complex configuration (multimodal, multiprofile, learned encoders) must be compared to justify its added cost. 3) Why it is the best first experiment: Under a limited budget, the single most informative first step is to anchor the search with a simple, fast, well-understood baseline. If config_009 performs well, all subsequent multimodal/multiprofile experiments must clearly beat it to be justified, sharply reducing the effective search space. If it performs poorly, we immediately know we must explore image or learned-encoder paths. Either outcome dramatically reduces uncertainty about the most important design axis (does RNA alone suffice?) and guides every subsequent experiment more efficiently than jumping into a complex multimodal configuration whose failure would be hard to attribute."
}

